<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/phonetics-and-the-ipa">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Linguistics, and it assumes the foundational
    lesson <em>How Language Works</em>, including its founding puzzle: spelling is not
    sound, and speech is the primary object of study. You need no phonetics background
    and no second language — your own mouth is the laboratory for most of this lesson.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe any consonant with the three coordinates of place, manner and voicing;</li>
    <li>read the vowel chart as a map of tongue height and backness;</li>
    <li>transcribe broadly in slashes and narrowly in brackets, and say which level a question needs;</li>
    <li>explain English spelling's chaos as frozen history rather than randomness;</li>
    <li>say precisely where phonetics ends and phonology begins, using the pin/spin case.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phonetics is <strong>the physics and physiology of speech</strong>: how the vocal tract
    produces sounds, what those sounds are physically, and how ears and brains process them.
    It has three branches with three objects of study — <strong>articulatory</strong> phonetics
    (what the mouth does), <strong>acoustic</strong> phonetics (what the air does), and
    <strong>auditory</strong> phonetics (what the listener's ear and brain do). You already
    know the founding puzzle from the foundational lessons: spelling is not sound. Letters
    are a bad map of speech, and worse in some languages than others. Phonetics is the
    discipline that draws the good map.
  </p>
  <p>
    The good map is the <strong>International Phonetic Alphabet</strong>, published in 1888
    by the International Phonetic Association and revised continuously since: one symbol per
    distinct speech sound, with brackets to mark transcription. The IPA is why a pronunciation
    dictionary works in any country, why speech therapists can write down what a child says,
    and why language teachers can describe a sound a learner has never heard. This lesson
    builds the map from the mouth outward, then runs into the place where phonetics hands
    over to phonology — the subject of the next lesson.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the IPA, the mouth-map, the spelling chaos, and the sounds beyond English" widget="equationStep" config={phonCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards form a route. Card 1 gives the
    principle (one symbol per sound); card 2 turns it into coordinates you can feel in
    your own mouth; card 3 explains why English spelling and the IPA disagree so
    violently; card 4 widens the lens past English entirely. Try card 2 against card 3:
    say "through" and "tough" and watch one identical letter string cover entirely
    different sounds.
  </p>

  <h2>Articulatory phonetics: the mouth as instrument</h2>
  <p>
    Every consonant can be located with three coordinates, and learning them turns the
    consonant chart into a map you can navigate rather than memorise. <strong>Place</strong>
    is where the constriction happens, running front to back: bilabial (both lips — p, b,
    m), labiodental (lip on teeth — f, v), dental (tongue on teeth — th of "think"),
    alveolar (tongue on the ridge behind the teeth — t, d, s, n), postalveolar (just behind
    the ridge — sh, ch, j), palatal (hard palate — y), velar (soft palate — k, g, ng),
    glottal (the vocal folds themselves — h). <strong>Manner</strong> is what kind of
    constriction: complete closure makes a <strong>stop</strong> (p, t, k), a narrow gap
    with turbulent airflow makes a <strong>fricative</strong> (s, f, sh), stop-then-friction
    makes an <strong>affricate</strong> (ch, j), air routed through the nose makes a
    <strong>nasal</strong> (m, n, ng), and open approximations are <strong>approximants</strong>
    (l, r, w, y). <strong>Voicing</strong> is whether the vocal folds vibrate during the
    constriction: s versus z, f versus v, t versus d.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: [p] is a voiceless bilabial stop — lips closed, folds still. Say "p" and
    hold it: silence between two closures. Now feel your throat on [b] — a hum before the
    release. That one difference (voicing) is the entire difference between "pat" and "bat",
    and English listeners are so trained on it that they struggle to hear anything else about
    those two sounds. Say "pin" with your palm in front of your mouth: you will feel a puff
    of air after the p. Say "spin": no puff. That puff is <strong>aspiration</strong>, a
    phonetic detail your mouth produces automatically — and it will matter at the end of
    this lesson.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "you can hear a word's sounds from its spelling,
  because letters are sounds." Letters are a historical convention, and a rough one:
  the "p" of "pin" and "spin" is two phonetically different sounds spelled the same,
  while "sh" needs two letters for one sound and "th" two letters for two different
  sounds. The IPA exists precisely because letters cannot be trusted to name sounds.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> consonants surrender to three coordinates — place, manner,
    voicing — and even automatic details like aspiration fall out of the system. Vowels
    resist that treatment, because they are shapes rather than constrictions.
  </p>

  <h2>Vowels: a two-dimensional map of resonance</h2>
  <p>
    Vowels have no constriction to classify; they are <em>shapes</em> of the vocal tract.
    Two coordinates suffice: <strong>tongue height</strong> (close versus open) and
    <strong>tongue backness</strong> (front versus back), plus one optional setting — lip
    rounding. That is why the standard vowel chart is shaped like a tilted mouth: its axes
    redraw the tongue's positions. English (British RP) runs roughly twelve monophthongs —
    the vowels of "beet", "bit", "bet", "bat", "father", "put", "boot", "but", "bird",
    "pot", "bought", "about" (the last being the central vowel <strong>schwa</strong> [ə],
    the most common sound in spoken English) — plus diphthongs that glide ("bite", "boy",
    "go"). Five vowel letters for that inventory is the arithmetic error at the heart of
    English spelling.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "English has five vowels, since the alphabet says
  so." The alphabet has five vowel <em>letters</em>; spoken English runs to roughly
  twelve monophthongs plus diphthongs, with schwa the most common sound of all. The
  mismatch between five letters and a dozen-plus sounds is not a curiosity — it is the
  engine of English spelling trouble.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> consonants have three coordinates and vowels a
    two-dimensional map, both drawn on the mouth. Now the notation that carries the map
    across languages: broad and narrow transcription.
  </p>

  <h2>The IPA in use: broad and narrow transcription</h2>
  <p>
    A few conventions carry most of the practical weight. Transcriptions between <strong>
    slashes</strong> (/pɪn/) are <em>broad</em>: they record only the sounds the language
    treats as meaning-bearing. Transcriptions between <strong>square brackets</strong>
    ([pʰɪn]) are <em>narrow</em>: they record actual phonetic detail, using diacritics —
    the small superscript ʰ marking aspiration, ̃ marking nasalisation, ː marking length.
    Both describe the same word; they answer different questions. A phonemic transcription
    says "this is the sound category"; a phonetic one says "this is what the mouth actually
    did". The example word [ðɪs] in the widget combines two sounds English spelling botches
    completely: the voiced dental fricative ð of "this" and the vowel of "kit" — neither
    has a reliable English letter.
  </p>

  <h2>Why English spelling is chaos: a historical accident</h2>
  <p>
    The chaos is not random — it is frozen history. When spelling conventions settled
    (Chaucer's era), English long vowels sat where their spellings suggest: "name" rhymed
    with "farm". Then the <strong>Great Vowel Shift</strong> — between roughly 1400 and
    1700 — raised and moved every long vowel: the "ah" of "name" climbed to "ay", the "ay"
    of "sea" climbed to "ee", and so on down the line. Printing arrived mid-shift and froze
    the old spellings onto new sounds. Add borrowed French spellings ("queue"), silent
    letters, and five letters chasing twenty vowel qualities, and you get the 26-letters/
    44-sounds mismatch. Every "ough" — through, though, tough, cough, bough, thought — is a
    fossil of a different history. Other writing systems froze at other moments: Spanish
    spelling tracks pronunciation rather well because its standardisation came later.
  </p>

  <h2>Sounds beyond English</h2>
  <p>
    English sits in the middle of the world's range. Some systems do things English mouths
    never learned: <strong>clicks</strong> (Xhosa and other southern African languages use
    ingressive click consonants — the "tsk" and horse-call sounds, running as ordinary
    consonants; Khoisan-area languages push inventories past 100 distinct sounds),
    <strong>ejectives</strong> and <strong>implosives</strong> (consonants powered by
    larynx motion rather than lung air — common in the Caucasus and the Americas),
    <strong>retroflexes</strong> (tongue curled back — routine in Hindi), and
    <strong>front rounded vowels</strong> (French "lune", German "schön" — English has no
    rounded front vowels, which is why those two are hard for English speakers). And
    roughly 40–50% of the world's languages are <strong>tone</strong> languages: pitch
    itself distinguishes words, so Mandarin mā, má, mǎ, mà are four different words
    (mother, hemp, horse, scold). The IPA notates all of it — clicks with ǀ ǁ ǂ, tones
    with diacritics — which is what makes it a genuinely universal alphabet rather than an
    English one with extra symbols.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "English spelling is chaotic because speakers or
  teachers have been careless." The chaos is frozen history with systematic causes: the
  Great Vowel Shift moved every long vowel between roughly 1400 and 1700 while printing
  froze the old spellings, borrowed French conventions piled on, and five letters were
  left covering twenty vowel qualities. Every "ough" is a fossil of a different
  history, not a random error.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the IPA covers the world's inventories, from clicks to
    tones, which proves it is a universal alphabet rather than an English one with
    extras. Now the boundary case where describing sounds stops and organising them
    begins.
  </p>

  <h2>Where phonetics shades into phonology</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the boundary case that opens the next lesson. Phonetically, English "pin" and
    "spin" contain two different p-sounds: aspirated [pʰ] and unaspirated [p]. The
    difference is measurable, teachable, real. Yet English speakers cannot hear it without
    training, and no two English words differ only by it — the language treats both as the
    same sound. Thai, by contrast, uses that exact difference to distinguish words, and
    Thai listeners hear the puff instantly. So what counts as "the same sound" is not a
    fact of acoustics; it is a fact of how a language <em>organises</em> acoustics. That
    organisational level — phonemes, allophones, rules — is phonology, and the aspiration
    of "pin" is its classic entry point.
  </p>

  <h2>The transcription workshop</h2>
  <p>
    Transcription is a skill with exercises, so here is the practice set. English "think"
    is /θɪŋk/ — the dental fricative θ with no English letter, then the nasal ŋ written
    with two letters. "Measure" is /ˈmɛʒə/ — the voiced fricative ʒ that English spelling
    never marks as its own sound, a stress mark ˈ before the first syllable, and schwa at
    the end. "Church" is /tʃɜːtʃ/ — an affricate written ch, the long central vowel with
    the length mark ː. Notice what the transcriptions capture that spelling cannot: stress,
    length, and sounds with no letter. Dictionaries use exactly this notation, which is why
    learning to read it pays for itself the first time you check a pronunciation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The skill scales beyond English in both directions. Reading: Japanese "sushi" is
    /sɯɕi/ — a vowel (ɯ) that is not English "oo", a fricative (ɕ) that is not quite "sh".
    Writing: the click in a Xhosa place-name, the tone contour on a Mandarin syllable, the
    breathy voice of Hindi consonants — all notated with dedicated symbols and diacritics.
    Two practical rules of thumb: transcribe what you hear, not what the spelling says
    (spelling is the enemy of transcription); and choose the level deliberately — broad
    phonemic transcription for teaching and dictionaries, narrow phonetic detail for accent
    work, therapy and research. The IPA's promise is that both levels have a symbol for
    everything you will ever need to write down.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the segment: stress, length, tone, intonation</h2>
  <p>
    Phonetics does not stop at individual sounds. The <strong>suprasegmental</strong> layer
    rides above them: <strong>stress</strong> (syllables made louder, longer, higher —
    English "REcord" the noun versus "reCORD" the verb, meaning fixed by stress alone),
    <strong>length</strong> (Japanese distinguishes words by vowel duration — "obasan"
    aunt versus "obaasan" grandmother), <strong>tone</strong> (the lexical pitches already
    met: Mandarin's four, Cantonese's six, plus register and contour systems across
    Africa and the Americas), and <strong>intonation</strong> — the sentence-level tune
    that distinguishes statement from question from sarcasm even when the words are
    identical. Intonation is why English speakers can hear a question without any question
    word, and why the same sentence read flatly sounds dead. These layers are phonetic in
    mechanism and phonological in organisation — every language picks which pitches,
    lengths and stresses carry meaning, the same selective move it makes with consonants.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Describe [s] in the three coordinates, then describe [z] and say what changed.</li>
    <li>Why is a vowel chart shaped like a mouth?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between /pɪn/ and [pʰɪn], and when would you choose each?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) [s] is a voiceless alveolar fricative: constriction
    at the alveolar ridge, turbulent airflow, folds still. [z] is identical except for
    voicing — the folds now buzz — which is the entire difference between "sip" and
    "zip". (2) Its two axes are tongue height and tongue backness, the coordinates that
    set a vowel's resonance, so the chart literally redraws the vowel space of the
    mouth. (3) Slashes mark a broad phonemic transcription — the sound category the
    language treats as meaning-bearing; brackets with the aspiration diacritic mark
    narrow phonetic detail — what the mouth actually did. Dictionaries and teaching use
    broad; accent work, therapy and research use narrow.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Phonetics studies speech in three branches: articulatory, acoustic and auditory.</li>
    <li>Any consonant is located by place, manner and voicing; the chart is a map of the mouth.</li>
    <li>Vowels are resonance shapes set by tongue height and backness, plus optional rounding.</li>
    <li>The IPA assigns one symbol per sound; slashes mark phonemic, brackets phonetic transcription.</li>
    <li>English spelling is frozen history: the Great Vowel Shift moved the vowels while spelling stood still.</li>
    <li>Sound inventories differ wildly across languages — clicks, ejectives, tones — and the IPA notates them all.</li>
    <li>Whether two sounds count as "the same" is a fact about a language's organisation — where phonology begins.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lesson <em>Phonology</em> takes exactly the pin/spin
    boundary case and builds phonemes, allophones and rules on it, while
    <em>Morphology</em> and <em>Syntax: Trees and Rules</em> cover the neighbouring
    layers. The advanced strand continues in <em>Historical Linguistics</em> (the Great
    Vowel Shift's full course) and <em>Psycholinguistics and Acquisition</em> (the
    listener's side).
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Acoustic phonetics explains what the ear is actually parsing. Voiced sounds begin as a
    <strong>source</strong> — the vocal folds buzzing at roughly 100–150 Hz for adult
    males, 180–220 Hz for adult females — and the throat, mouth and nose act as a
    <strong>filter</strong>, resonating at characteristic frequencies called
    <strong>formants</strong>. The first two formants (F1, F2) track vowel height and
    backness almost linearly, which is why vowels plot as tidy points on a formant chart —
    the acoustic version of the mouth map. Consonants appear on a spectrogram as events:
    stops as silences with a burst, fricatives as bands of noise. This is source-filter
    theory, and it is why speech is machine-readable: a spectrogram is a picture of the
    grammar of the mouth, and everything from hearing-aid design to speech recognition to
    voice synthesis is applied formant thinking. Auditory phonetics completes the chain —
    the cochlea's frequency mapping explains why some contrasts (s versus sh) are
    perceptually robust and others (subtle vowel differences) are language-specific —
    and it is the layer where phonetics finally stops being physics and starts being
    perception, which is phonology's home turf.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field also has its instrumentation, worth knowing because it explains where the
    numbers come from. <strong>Palatography</strong> dusts the tongue or palate to show
    exactly where contact occurs; <strong>electromagnetic articulography</strong> tracks
    sensors glued to tongue and lips in three dimensions at hundreds of frames a second;
    <strong>ultrasound</strong> films the tongue's shape live; laryngoscopy watches the
    vocal folds themselves. Out of these come the empirical surprises: the tongue reaches
    vowel targets differently in fast versus careful speech without losing the contrast
    ("target undershoot" — the listener compensates), and infants babble consonants in a
    universal order (labials and front sounds first) that tracks how far the articulators
    have to travel. Speech technology is applied phonetics end to end: text-to-speech
    systems synthesise formant trajectories and prosodic tunes; automatic speech
    recognition learned from spectrograms; and hearing-aid and cochlear-implant design
    is tuned to the exact frequency regions where English (or Mandarin, or Spanish)
    contrasts live. When a product "understands" you, a century of phonetics — from the
    1888 alphabet to the formant charts — is doing the listening.
  </p>
